-260830-jeaivu`.\n\n## What this round closed\n\n**F1 — symlink stance.** The seams now go opposite ways, deliberately and declared:\n\n| Seam | Stance | Why |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Read (`Load`/`LoadOS`) | follows symlinks, kind-checks the resolved target | pinned SPEC resolves-then-checks; refusing a symlinked XDG root was an invented constraint |\n| Mutating (`Migrate`/`MigrateOS`) | no-follow | an atomic rename replaces the operator's *link*, not the document; SPEC silent → fail closed |\n\nYou told me to decide the config-file question with a citation rather than assume: I took the SPEC.md:2648 resolve-then-require-regular idiom over the stricter reading of :738, so a config file symlinked onto a regular file **loads**. Stated in the README paragraph on loader kind checks, pinned in both directions at the real entries.\n\n**Bonus production fix:** `replaceDurably` now checks the selected file's kind/mode *before* any durable write. It used to publish `config.toml.bak.<version>` and *then* refuse a symlinked source — a refused migration mutated the directory.\n\n**F2** — `MigrateOS` 0.0% → 100.0%, driven end-to-end against a real directory tree. **F3** — `(*MigrationError).Error` 0.0% → 100.0%, extended the existing no-leak test as instructed plus a real-production assertion.\n\n## The thing you should actually read\n\n**One mutant survived on first run, and it was a real gap.** M27 — `MigrateOS` discarding its own `OSInputs` error — stayed green because my test asserted only `errors.Is(err, ErrInvalidContext)`, and `ResolvePaths` refuses the same platform later with the same sentinel. Gate present, reachable, proving nothing. Fixed by asserting the refusing `Operation` (`\"capture OS inputs\"` vs the downstream `\"validate inputs\"`); it then died. Logged rather than quietly patched — it's the \"subsumed downstream\" shape, not \"gate missing\".\n\nAlso honest about M25: deleting the source-inspection guard leaves `info` nil, so it dies by panic rather than a